{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQjrX3hxMJpDMo98nDjPHo","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer R1, focusing on methodology soundness and experimental rigor.\n\n---\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper proposes a dual-constrained adaptive method for selecting the weighting exponent b* in the detect-and-reweight framework w=(1-q)^b for pretraining on contaminated web corpora. The core idea is to find the minimum b satisfying an effective authenticity rate threshold (alpha_eff >= tau) subject to an effective sample size floor (ESS >= n_min), framed as a \"minimum intervention principle.\" While the problem is practically relevant and the per-batch adaptive approach is sensible, the experimental evidence reveals very small effect sizes (0.04 PPL at the primary setting), a remarkably flat loss landscape that undermines the motivation for sophisticated adaptation, and several critical experiments that remain incomplete or are reported on single seeds only.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model grid search (b=10 for small models, b=1 for 1.7B) is a genuine practical limitation. Automating this selection with a principled rule is useful.\n\n- **Correct mathematical treatment of the dual-constraint structure.** The Q1 analysis (Section on alpha_eff vs ESS equivalence) correctly identifies that the optimization is inf{b : alpha_eff >= tau}, not max{b : ESS >= n_min}. The monotonicity of alpha_eff(b) guarantees existence and uniqueness of b_tau. The empirical finding that ~40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding (b* = 1.67 vs ESS-only b* = 4.15) is the strongest evidence that the two constraints are genuinely distinct. This refutes the naive claim that the framework degenerates to pure ESS thresholding.\n\n- **Thorough diagnostic culture.** The project demonstrates exceptional rigor in tracking confounds: identifying checkpoint resume artifacts (D026), diagnosing the n_min=3 failure mode (D012), separating eval entries from training entries in step_metrics, and maintaining a full decision log. The invalidation of seed456 b>=2.0 results rather than silently including them is commendable.\n\n- **b* distribution stability across seeds.** Table 4 in the experiment summary shows b* statistics (mean, median, percentiles) with CV < 2.5% across three seeds. This is convincing evidence that the adaptive rule produces deterministic, data-driven behavior rather than noise-driven variation.\n\n- **Lightweight detector sufficiency is a genuinely useful finding.** The deliberate choice of TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) over ModernBERT (AUC=0.986) after discovering q-score polarization (D005-D008) is methodologically sound and produces a stronger paper narrative. The imagined detector ablation showing only 0.005 PPL difference between the two detectors supports the claim that calibration matters more than raw AUC.\n\n- **Honest calibration of claims.** The project internally acknowledges that tau is an empirical hyperparameter (D025), not a proportion-free theoretical derivation. The framing as \"empirical hyperparameter with theoretical motivation\" is appropriate.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The flat b-PPL landscape severely undermines the value proposition.** Table 2 (extended grid sweep) shows a PPL range of only 0.014 across b in [0.1, 0.5]. The paper's own experiment summary confirms \"PPL remarkably flat across b in [0.1, 0.5].\" If any fixed b in this wide range produces nearly identical results, the practical value of a per-batch adaptive b* that oscillates around median 0.87 is questionable. A reviewer can legitimately argue: \"just set b=0.3 and move on.\" The paper needs to directly confront this, perhaps by showing settings where the landscape is NOT flat (e.g., higher contamination rates) where adaptation genuinely matters.\n\n- **(Major) ESS-only baseline is not yet completed.** As of the project state, ess_only_seed42 is still running (~25% complete). This is the single most critical experiment for Claim 1. The paper's central narrative -- that minimum intervention (alpha_eff + ESS) outperforms maximum intervention (ESS-only) -- has zero direct training evidence. The Q1 analysis shows the two methods diverge in b* selection (1.67 vs 4.15 when alpha_eff binds), but whether this divergence translates to measurable PPL differences is unknown. Table 3 in the paper summary reports the imagined result as 0.01 PPL difference (17.65 vs 17.66), which would be statistically indistinguishable. If this materializes, the alpha_eff component adds conceptual complexity without measurable benefit.\n\n- **(Major) Effect sizes are at or below noise level.** The core improvement is 0.042 mean PPL (17.693 -> 17.651) over uniform, and the adaptive-vs-grid-best gap is 0.005 PPL. The seed variance (std ~0.007-0.009 across seeds for each method) is of the same order as the claimed improvements. No formal statistical significance tests (paired t-tests, confidence intervals) are reported for the primary comparison. The \"aggregate ratio\" metric of 88.6% (less than the originally targeted 90%) was passed by changing the acceptance criterion post-hoc (D019), which is concerning from a pre-registration standpoint.\n\n- **(Major) Tau sweep and several critical ablations are incomplete.** At the time of review, tau_06_seed42 is at 3% progress, and tau=0.5/0.7/0.9/0.95 are in PREPARING status. Without the tau sweep, Claim 3's assertion that \"any reasonable tau works\" is an unsupported assertion. The n_min sensitivity curve has only two operative points (n_min=2 works, n_min=3 fails at batch_size=4). The batch_size=8/16 experiments have not started. These are not minor gaps -- they directly address the paper's most predictable reviewer concerns.\n\n- **(Major) The \"two hyperparameters replacing one\" problem is inadequately addressed.** The method replaces Drayson's single hyperparameter b with two hyperparameters (tau, n_min) plus algorithmic choices (b_max=20.0, resolution=1000). The n_min=3 -> n_min=2 change was the difference between complete method failure and success on seed42 (D012). The paper's anticipated response -- that tau and n_min are \"dataset-agnostic robust defaults\" -- requires the tau sweep and batch_size ablation that are currently missing. As it stands, n_min=2 works for batch_size=4 but the project's own analysis (D015) notes that \"batch_size=4 + n_min=2 puts ESS dynamic range at only [2, 4], with 75%+ batches hitting ESS floor ~2.0.\" This suggests the method is operating at a degenerate boundary, not in a robust operating regime.\n\n- **(Minor) Contamination is synthetic and uniform by design.** All completed experiments use Qwen3-0.6B generating synthetic text injected at a known, uniform 40% ratio. The domain-skewed and cross-generator experiments are imagined but not executed. Real web contamination is neither uniform in domain nor from a single generator family. The ecological validity of the experimental setup is limited.\n\n- **(Minor) Only one model scale has completed results.** The Llama-3.2-3B experiments (Table 9) showing 2x relative improvement are imagined, not real. The GPU budget decision (D024) descoped this to P1. The paper's claim of cross-model generalization rests on imagined data.\n\n- **(Minor) The concentration bound (compute_concentration_bound in alpha_eff.py) is implemented but not discussed.** This could strengthen the theoretical contribution if it provides finite-sample guarantees on alpha_eff estimation quality. Its omission is a missed opportunity.\n\n- **(Minor) Grid-best at b=0.5 is at the search boundary.** The experiment plan to extend to b=0.1/0.2/0.3 was deprioritized (D027) after repeated SIGTERM kills. Without confirming the grid-best location, there is a residual risk that the true optimum is at b<0.5, which would change the aggregate ratio calculation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **The critical question:** If the ESS-only baseline (max{b : ESS >= n_min}) produces PPL within 0.01 of the dual-constrained method, what is the independent contribution of the alpha_eff constraint? Would you concede that the method reduces to ESS thresholding in practice, even if the two differ in b* values?\n\n2. The find_optimal_b function (alpha_eff.py lines 81-137) uses a grid of 1000 points followed by bisection. At batch_size=4, this means 1000 ESS + 1000 alpha_eff evaluations per micro-batch. What is the wall-clock overhead of b* computation relative to the forward/backward pass? Is this negligible at scale?\n\n3. The project internally discovered that the original tau formula depended on contamination_ratio (tau = 1.0 - contamination_ratio * 0.5, line 278-279 of pretrain_weighted.py, per D021). This was fixed to a hard-coded tau=0.8. Has the paper been updated to reflect this history, or does it present tau=0.8 as if it were always the design? Transparency about this iteration would strengthen credibility.\n\n4. Table 4 shows b*=0 for ~50% of micro-batches (the \"clean\" batches where alpha_eff(0) >= tau). This means half the training steps use uniform weighting. Is the 0.04 PPL improvement driven entirely by the ~50% of batches where b*>0? If so, the per-sample effect magnitude is actually ~0.08 PPL on the affected batches, which is more meaningful than the diluted 0.04.\n\n5. The imagined downstream results (Table 12: MMLU +0.15, HellaSwag +0.30) are described as \"not significant\" and \"marginal.\" If the method cannot produce statistically significant downstream improvements at 200M tokens, what is the minimum training scale where you would expect significance?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Formal significance testing for the main results.** Paired t-tests or bootstrap confidence intervals across the three seeds for adaptive vs. grid-best and adaptive vs. uniform. The current reporting (means and per-seed values) is insufficient for a rigorous claim.\n\n2. **ESS-only baseline with at least 3 seeds.** This is the most important missing experiment. A single seed (even when it completes) cannot establish whether the alpha_eff constraint provides a statistically significant advantage.\n\n3. **Complete tau sweep with at least 5 values and 3 seeds each.** Currently zero tau sweep data points exist from actual training runs.\n\n4. **Batch_size=8 and batch_size=16 experiments.** The current method operates in a degenerate regime (ESS range [2,4]) at batch_size=4. Demonstrating that the method works in a more typical operating regime (ESS range [2, 16]) is essential.\n\n5. **Wall-clock overhead measurement.** The paper should quantify the computational cost of the b* search per batch.\n\n6. **A \"fixed b=0.3\" baseline.** Given the flat landscape, comparing adaptive b* against a single naively chosen fixed b (e.g., b=0.3 or b=0.5) with zero tuning cost would clarify the marginal value of adaptation.\n\n7. **Learning curve analysis.** Does the adaptive method's advantage grow or shrink over training? Early vs. late training b* distributions could reveal whether adaptation is most valuable during specific training phases.\n\n### Claim-by-Claim Assessment\n\n- **Claim 1 (Principled dual-constrained framework):** Partially supported. The mathematical formulation is correct, and the Q1 analysis demonstrates that alpha_eff and ESS constraints are genuinely distinct (40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding). However, the ESS-only head-to-head comparison -- the single most important experiment -- is incomplete. Without it, the claim that minimum intervention outperforms maximum intervention is theoretical, not empirical. The word \"principled\" is overclaimed given that tau is an empirical hyperparameter and the convergence theory connection (arXiv 2602.16065) is motivational only.\n\n- **Claim 2 (Adaptive matches grid-best, eliminates tuning cost):** Mostly supported with caveats. The 3-seed results show adaptive within 0.005-0.01 PPL of grid-best across all seeds, which is within noise. The aggregate capture ratio of 88.6% (below the original 90% target, passed via post-hoc criterion revision) is honestly reported. The main caveat is that the flat PPL landscape (0.014 range across b in [0.1, 0.5]) means any fixed b in this range also \"matches grid-best\" without adaptation. The claim should be weakened to: \"adaptive provides a reasonable default that is competitive with grid-best, but the flat landscape means simple fixed choices work nearly as well in this setting.\"\n\n- **Claim 3 (No known contamination ratio or clean validation set needed):** Partially supported after the tau fix (D021). The b* derivation genuinely does not use contamination_ratio at runtime. However, the claim that tau is insensitive requires the tau sweep, which is incomplete. The D025 audit correctly identified that the theoretical motivation for tau requires known proportions, and the honest downgrade to \"empirical hyperparameter\" is appropriate. The claim would be strengthened by showing the method works across tau in [0.6, 0.8] without significant degradation.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a real problem and the dual-constraint formulation is mathematically sound. However, five factors prevent a positive recommendation: (1) the core differentiating experiment (ESS-only baseline) is incomplete, leaving Claim 1's central argument without empirical support; (2) the flat b-PPL landscape undermines the practical motivation for sophisticated adaptation; (3) effect sizes are at noise level without formal significance testing; (4) critical ablations (tau sweep, batch_size, complete n_min curve) are missing; and (5) the method introduces two hyperparameters where n_min was empirically shown to be fragile (method failure at n_min=3). The work has the ingredients for a solid contribution if the ESS-only baseline shows a clear advantage, the tau sweep confirms robustness, and larger-scale experiments (higher contamination, larger models) demonstrate amplified effects. In its current state, it reads as a promising work-in-progress rather than a complete contribution ready for a top venue. A workshop paper or short paper at the current evidence level would be appropriate; a full paper requires completing the critical experimental gaps."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac093fc3c49c80798 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac093fc3c49c80798' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 92600\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 141169</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd81513d-34ac-46ce-a706-72967144b268","uuid":"19106bb9-2cc0-4115-84f4-c276c93b43ee","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:10:45.334Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Adaptive Provenance-Based Reweighting for Pretraining on Contaminated Web Corpora\n\n**Core idea:** Web corpora contain 20-60% machine-generated text. Prior work (Drayson et al. 2025) uses w_i = (1-q_i)^b with detector scores q_i, but b requires per-model grid search. This paper proposes dual-constrained adaptive b* derived per-batch from two constraints:\n1. Quality lower bound: α_eff(b) ≥ τ (effective real-data fraction must exceed threshold)\n2. Stability upper bound: ESS(b) ≥ n_min (Kish effective sample size floor)\n\nSolution: b* = inf{b ≥ 0 : α_eff(b) ≥ τ} s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min — \"minimum intervention principle\"\n\n**Key claims:**\n1. Principled dual-constrained framework grounded in minimum-intervention principle\n2. Adaptive b* matches grid-search-optimal b within seed variance across models (0.6B, 3B), contamination ratios (40%, 60%), batch sizes (4, 8, 16), eliminating tuning cost (6N→1 runs)\n3. Lightweight TF-IDF+LR detector (AUC=0.939) suffices; method needs no known contamination ratio or clean validation set\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n\nTable 1 (Main, Qwen3-0.6B, ρ=0.4, 200M tokens):\n- Uniform: 17.693 mean PPL\n- Grid-best (b=0.3): 17.644\n- Adaptive (dual): 17.651\n- Adaptive/Grid gap: 0.007 mean\n\nTable 2 (Extended grid sweep): PPL remarkably flat across b∈[0.1,0.5], range only 0.014\n\nTable 3 (Dual vs ESS-only, seed42):\n- Dual: PPL 17.65, b* median 0.88, b* std 1.93, grad var 1.00×\n- ESS-only: PPL 17.66, b* median 3.20, b* std 3.80, grad var 1.35×\n- Difference: 0.01 PPL — statistically indistinguishable\n\nTable 4 (Full baseline comparison, seed42):\n- Random reweight: +0.005 (confirms detector signal matters)\n- Detect-and-remove: -0.005 (hard filtering ineffective)\n- Golden Ratio oracle (requires known ρ): 17.640 (only 0.01 better than adaptive)\n\nTable 5 (τ sweep): Robust in [0.6, 0.8], degrades at boundaries (τ=0.5: under-reweight, τ≥0.9: over-reweight)\n\nTable 6 (n_min sensitivity, batch_size=4): Sharp cliff at n_min=3, n_min≥4 collapses to uniform\n\nTable 7 (Batch size): bs=8 and bs=16 → PPL 17.64, n_min knife-edge vanishes\n\nTable 8 (ρ=0.6): Uniform degrades super-linearly (17.69→18.50). Adaptive improvement amplifies only 1.25× (ESS tightens)\n\nTable 9 (Cross-model): Llama-3.2-3B shows 2× relative improvement (0.444% vs 0.226%), b* median nearly identical (0.85 vs 0.88)\n\nTable 10 (Detector quality): ModernBERT AUC 0.986 vs TF-IDF 0.939 → only 0.005 PPL difference\n\nTable 11 (Cross-generator): Improvement drops from 0.04 to 0.03 PPL\n\nTable 12 (Downstream): MMLU +0.15 (not significant), HellaSwag +0.30 (marginal)\n\nDomain-skewed contamination: improvement drops to 0.02 PPL (~50% of uniform-injection ratio)\n\n**Experimental design reference (from review_context.yaml):** The full experimental design includes these imagined experiments with expected results:\n- Extended grid-b low (b=0.1/0.2/0.3) across 3 seeds — confirms flat PPL landscape\n- ρ=0.6 experiments — effect amplification limited (1.25×)\n- n_min ablation (1-5) — knife-edge at n_min=3 is batch_size=4 artifact\n- ESS-only baseline — Δ=0.01 vs dual, statistically indistinguishable\n- τ sweep — robust in [0.6, 0.8], boundary degradation\n- Golden Ratio oracle — adaptive approaches oracle without knowing ρ\n- Batch size 8/16 — n_min knife-edge vanishes, PPL converges at bs=8\n- ModernBERT detector — AUC +5% yields only 0.005 PPL\n- Cross-generator (Llama-3.2-1B) — improvement drops to 0.03\n- Llama-3.2-3B cross-model — 2× relative improvement, b* distribution stable\n- Detect-and-remove baseline — largely ineffective\n- Random reweighting — confirms detector signal is necessary\n- Domain-skewed (code-heavy) — method's weakest setting (~50% ratio)\n- Downstream eval (MMLU/HellaSwag) — not significant at 200M tokens\n\nThe paper covers most of these experiments. Check coverage completeness.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the dual-constraint formulation correct? Is the minimum-intervention principle well-motivated? Are there flaws in the mathematical framework?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Statistical significance, number of seeds, baselines, ablations, hyperparameter sensitivity coverage\n3. **Claim-evidence alignment**: Do the experimental results actually support the three stated claims? Are claims overclaimed or appropriately calibrated?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Weaknesses in experimental design**: What's missing? What could undermine the conclusions?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n### Claim-by-Claim Assessment\n- Claim 1: ...\n- Claim 2: ...\n- Claim 3: ...\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"ac093fc3c49c80798","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer R1, focusing on methodology soundness and experimental rigor.\n\n---\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper proposes a dual-constrained adaptive method for selecting the weighting exponent b* in the detect-and-reweight framework w=(1-q)^b for pretraining on contaminated web corpora. The core idea is to find the minimum b satisfying an effective authenticity rate threshold (alpha_eff >= tau) subject to an effective sample size floor (ESS >= n_min), framed as a \"minimum intervention principle.\" While the problem is practically relevant and the per-batch adaptive approach is sensible, the experimental evidence reveals very small effect sizes (0.04 PPL at the primary setting), a remarkably flat loss landscape that undermines the motivation for sophisticated adaptation, and several critical experiments that remain incomplete or are reported on single seeds only.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model grid search (b=10 for small models, b=1 for 1.7B) is a genuine practical limitation. Automating this selection with a principled rule is useful.\n\n- **Correct mathematical treatment of the dual-constraint structure.** The Q1 analysis (Section on alpha_eff vs ESS equivalence) correctly identifies that the optimization is inf{b : alpha_eff >= tau}, not max{b : ESS >= n_min}. The monotonicity of alpha_eff(b) guarantees existence and uniqueness of b_tau. The empirical finding that ~40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding (b* = 1.67 vs ESS-only b* = 4.15) is the strongest evidence that the two constraints are genuinely distinct. This refutes the naive claim that the framework degenerates to pure ESS thresholding.\n\n- **Thorough diagnostic culture.** The project demonstrates exceptional rigor in tracking confounds: identifying checkpoint resume artifacts (D026), diagnosing the n_min=3 failure mode (D012), separating eval entries from training entries in step_metrics, and maintaining a full decision log. The invalidation of seed456 b>=2.0 results rather than silently including them is commendable.\n\n- **b* distribution stability across seeds.** Table 4 in the experiment summary shows b* statistics (mean, median, percentiles) with CV < 2.5% across three seeds. This is convincing evidence that the adaptive rule produces deterministic, data-driven behavior rather than noise-driven variation.\n\n- **Lightweight detector sufficiency is a genuinely useful finding.** The deliberate choice of TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) over ModernBERT (AUC=0.986) after discovering q-score polarization (D005-D008) is methodologically sound and produces a stronger paper narrative. The imagined detector ablation showing only 0.005 PPL difference between the two detectors supports the claim that calibration matters more than raw AUC.\n\n- **Honest calibration of claims.** The project internally acknowledges that tau is an empirical hyperparameter (D025), not a proportion-free theoretical derivation. The framing as \"empirical hyperparameter with theoretical motivation\" is appropriate.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The flat b-PPL landscape severely undermines the value proposition.** Table 2 (extended grid sweep) shows a PPL range of only 0.014 across b in [0.1, 0.5]. The paper's own experiment summary confirms \"PPL remarkably flat across b in [0.1, 0.5].\" If any fixed b in this wide range produces nearly identical results, the practical value of a per-batch adaptive b* that oscillates around median 0.87 is questionable. A reviewer can legitimately argue: \"just set b=0.3 and move on.\" The paper needs to directly confront this, perhaps by showing settings where the landscape is NOT flat (e.g., higher contamination rates) where adaptation genuinely matters.\n\n- **(Major) ESS-only baseline is not yet completed.** As of the project state, ess_only_seed42 is still running (~25% complete). This is the single most critical experiment for Claim 1. The paper's central narrative -- that minimum intervention (alpha_eff + ESS) outperforms maximum intervention (ESS-only) -- has zero direct training evidence. The Q1 analysis shows the two methods diverge in b* selection (1.67 vs 4.15 when alpha_eff binds), but whether this divergence translates to measurable PPL differences is unknown. Table 3 in the paper summary reports the imagined result as 0.01 PPL difference (17.65 vs 17.66), which would be statistically indistinguishable. If this materializes, the alpha_eff component adds conceptual complexity without measurable benefit.\n\n- **(Major) Effect sizes are at or below noise level.** The core improvement is 0.042 mean PPL (17.693 -> 17.651) over uniform, and the adaptive-vs-grid-best gap is 0.005 PPL. The seed variance (std ~0.007-0.009 across seeds for each method) is of the same order as the claimed improvements. No formal statistical significance tests (paired t-tests, confidence intervals) are reported for the primary comparison. The \"aggregate ratio\" metric of 88.6% (less than the originally targeted 90%) was passed by changing the acceptance criterion post-hoc (D019), which is concerning from a pre-registration standpoint.\n\n- **(Major) Tau sweep and several critical ablations are incomplete.** At the time of review, tau_06_seed42 is at 3% progress, and tau=0.5/0.7/0.9/0.95 are in PREPARING status. Without the tau sweep, Claim 3's assertion that \"any reasonable tau works\" is an unsupported assertion. The n_min sensitivity curve has only two operative points (n_min=2 works, n_min=3 fails at batch_size=4). The batch_size=8/16 experiments have not started. These are not minor gaps -- they directly address the paper's most predictable reviewer concerns.\n\n- **(Major) The \"two hyperparameters replacing one\" problem is inadequately addressed.** The method replaces Drayson's single hyperparameter b with two hyperparameters (tau, n_min) plus algorithmic choices (b_max=20.0, resolution=1000). The n_min=3 -> n_min=2 change was the difference between complete method failure and success on seed42 (D012). The paper's anticipated response -- that tau and n_min are \"dataset-agnostic robust defaults\" -- requires the tau sweep and batch_size ablation that are currently missing. As it stands, n_min=2 works for batch_size=4 but the project's own analysis (D015) notes that \"batch_size=4 + n_min=2 puts ESS dynamic range at only [2, 4], with 75%+ batches hitting ESS floor ~2.0.\" This suggests the method is operating at a degenerate boundary, not in a robust operating regime.\n\n- **(Minor) Contamination is synthetic and uniform by design.** All completed experiments use Qwen3-0.6B generating synthetic text injected at a known, uniform 40% ratio. The domain-skewed and cross-generator experiments are imagined but not executed. Real web contamination is neither uniform in domain nor from a single generator family. The ecological validity of the experimental setup is limited.\n\n- **(Minor) Only one model scale has completed results.** The Llama-3.2-3B experiments (Table 9) showing 2x relative improvement are imagined, not real. The GPU budget decision (D024) descoped this to P1. The paper's claim of cross-model generalization rests on imagined data.\n\n- **(Minor) The concentration bound (compute_concentration_bound in alpha_eff.py) is implemented but not discussed.** This could strengthen the theoretical contribution if it provides finite-sample guarantees on alpha_eff estimation quality. Its omission is a missed opportunity.\n\n- **(Minor) Grid-best at b=0.5 is at the search boundary.** The experiment plan to extend to b=0.1/0.2/0.3 was deprioritized (D027) after repeated SIGTERM kills. Without confirming the grid-best location, there is a residual risk that the true optimum is at b<0.5, which would change the aggregate ratio calculation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **The critical question:** If the ESS-only baseline (max{b : ESS >= n_min}) produces PPL within 0.01 of the dual-constrained method, what is the independent contribution of the alpha_eff constraint? Would you concede that the method reduces to ESS thresholding in practice, even if the two differ in b* values?\n\n2. The find_optimal_b function (alpha_eff.py lines 81-137) uses a grid of 1000 points followed by bisection. At batch_size=4, this means 1000 ESS + 1000 alpha_eff evaluations per micro-batch. What is the wall-clock overhead of b* computation relative to the forward/backward pass? Is this negligible at scale?\n\n3. The project internally discovered that the original tau formula depended on contamination_ratio (tau = 1.0 - contamination_ratio * 0.5, line 278-279 of pretrain_weighted.py, per D021). This was fixed to a hard-coded tau=0.8. Has the paper been updated to reflect this history, or does it present tau=0.8 as if it were always the design? Transparency about this iteration would strengthen credibility.\n\n4. Table 4 shows b*=0 for ~50% of micro-batches (the \"clean\" batches where alpha_eff(0) >= tau). This means half the training steps use uniform weighting. Is the 0.04 PPL improvement driven entirely by the ~50% of batches where b*>0? If so, the per-sample effect magnitude is actually ~0.08 PPL on the affected batches, which is more meaningful than the diluted 0.04.\n\n5. The imagined downstream results (Table 12: MMLU +0.15, HellaSwag +0.30) are described as \"not significant\" and \"marginal.\" If the method cannot produce statistically significant downstream improvements at 200M tokens, what is the minimum training scale where you would expect significance?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Formal significance testing for the main results.** Paired t-tests or bootstrap confidence intervals across the three seeds for adaptive vs. grid-best and adaptive vs. uniform. The current reporting (means and per-seed values) is insufficient for a rigorous claim.\n\n2. **ESS-only baseline with at least 3 seeds.** This is the most important missing experiment. A single seed (even when it completes) cannot establish whether the alpha_eff constraint provides a statistically significant advantage.\n\n3. **Complete tau sweep with at least 5 values and 3 seeds each.** Currently zero tau sweep data points exist from actual training runs.\n\n4. **Batch_size=8 and batch_size=16 experiments.** The current method operates in a degenerate regime (ESS range [2,4]) at batch_size=4. Demonstrating that the method works in a more typical operating regime (ESS range [2, 16]) is essential.\n\n5. **Wall-clock overhead measurement.** The paper should quantify the computational cost of the b* search per batch.\n\n6. **A \"fixed b=0.3\" baseline.** Given the flat landscape, comparing adaptive b* against a single naively chosen fixed b (e.g., b=0.3 or b=0.5) with zero tuning cost would clarify the marginal value of adaptation.\n\n7. **Learning curve analysis.** Does the adaptive method's advantage grow or shrink over training? Early vs. late training b* distributions could reveal whether adaptation is most valuable during specific training phases.\n\n### Claim-by-Claim Assessment\n\n- **Claim 1 (Principled dual-constrained framework):** Partially supported. The mathematical formulation is correct, and the Q1 analysis demonstrates that alpha_eff and ESS constraints are genuinely distinct (40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding). However, the ESS-only head-to-head comparison -- the single most important experiment -- is incomplete. Without it, the claim that minimum intervention outperforms maximum intervention is theoretical, not empirical. The word \"principled\" is overclaimed given that tau is an empirical hyperparameter and the convergence theory connection (arXiv 2602.16065) is motivational only.\n\n- **Claim 2 (Adaptive matches grid-best, eliminates tuning cost):** Mostly supported with caveats. The 3-seed results show adaptive within 0.005-0.01 PPL of grid-best across all seeds, which is within noise. The aggregate capture ratio of 88.6% (below the original 90% target, passed via post-hoc criterion revision) is honestly reported. The main caveat is that the flat PPL landscape (0.014 range across b in [0.1, 0.5]) means any fixed b in this range also \"matches grid-best\" without adaptation. The claim should be weakened to: \"adaptive provides a reasonable default that is competitive with grid-best, but the flat landscape means simple fixed choices work nearly as well in this setting.\"\n\n- **Claim 3 (No known contamination ratio or clean validation set needed):** Partially supported after the tau fix (D021). The b* derivation genuinely does not use contamination_ratio at runtime. However, the claim that tau is insensitive requires the tau sweep, which is incomplete. The D025 audit correctly identified that the theoretical motivation for tau requires known proportions, and the honest downgrade to \"empirical hyperparameter\" is appropriate. The claim would be strengthened by showing the method works across tau in [0.6, 0.8] without significant degradation.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a real problem and the dual-constraint formulation is mathematically sound. However, five factors prevent a positive recommendation: (1) the core differentiating experiment (ESS-only baseline) is incomplete, leaving Claim 1's central argument without empirical support; (2) the flat b-PPL landscape undermines the practical motivation for sophisticated adaptation; (3) effect sizes are at noise level without formal significance testing; (4) critical ablations (tau sweep, batch_size, complete n_min curve) are missing; and (5) the method introduces two hyperparameters where n_min was empirically shown to be fragile (method failure at n_min=3). The work has the ingredients for a solid contribution if the ESS-only baseline shows a clear advantage, the tau sweep confirms robustness, and larger-scale experiments (higher contamination, larger models) demonstrate amplified effects. In its current state, it reads as a promising work-in-progress rather than a complete contribution ready for a top venue. A workshop paper or short paper at the current evidence level would be appropriate; a full paper requires completing the critical experimental gaps."}],"totalDurationMs":141169,"totalTokens":92600,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":707,"cache_read_input_tokens":88301,"output_tokens":3589,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":707},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3589,"cache_read_input_tokens":88301,"cache_creation_input_tokens":707,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":707,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
