{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ANGBXxKQAKoxoZu2GDttXi","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper proposes a dual-constrained adaptive method for selecting the weight exponent b* in the detect-and-reweight framework w=(1-q)^b for pretraining on contaminated web corpora. The method uses an alpha_eff quality lower bound and an ESS stability upper bound to derive b* per-batch via bisection, eliminating the per-model grid search required by Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025). While the \"minimum intervention principle\" framing is intellectually appealing, the empirical evidence reveals an extremely flat PPL landscape across b values (total range 0.165 over b=0.5 to 5.0, with the actionable range b=0.1-0.5 spanning roughly 0.014 PPL), making it difficult to argue that the choice of b matters enough to warrant a principled selection framework.\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n- **Well-identified practical problem.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model grid search (b=10 for small models, b=1 for 1.7B) is a genuine pain point, and eliminating this tuning cost is a useful contribution for practitioners.\n\n- **Clean mathematical formulation.** The dual-constraint framework (alpha_eff as lower bound, ESS as upper bound, inf{b} as minimum intervention) is elegant and clearly presented. The monotonicity proof for alpha_eff(b) and the resulting guarantee of existence/uniqueness of b_tau are sound.\n\n- **Rigorous internal diagnostics.** The project demonstrates unusually thorough self-scrutiny: the D020-D023 decision chain (discovering alpha_eff may degenerate to ESS thresholding, correcting the overcorrection, verifying that ~40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding) is commendable. The Q1 empirical analysis (49.9% clean, 20.2% alpha_eff binding, 30.0% ESS binding among all micro-batches) is exactly the kind of evidence needed to defend the dual-constraint claim.\n\n- **Cross-seed stability of b* distribution.** The b* distribution statistics are remarkably stable across seeds (CV < 2.5% for all moments), demonstrating that the adaptive method is deterministic with respect to data composition rather than sensitive to training randomness. This is a genuinely useful property.\n\n- **Lightweight detector sufficiency.** Demonstrating that TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) suffices where Drayson uses ModernBERT 150M (AUC=0.986) is a practically valuable finding. The insight that calibration matters more than classification accuracy for continuous reweighting is well-supported.\n\n- **Honest treatment of limitations.** The paper (as framed) acknowledges that dual-constraint and ESS-only are empirically indistinguishable in PPL, that tau is an empirical hyperparameter (not theoretically derived in a proportion-free manner), and that downstream improvements are not statistically significant at 200M tokens.\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity)\n\n- **[Major] The flat PPL landscape undermines the core motivation.** PPL is monotonically increasing in b with a total range of only 0.165 across b=0.5 to 5.0 (17.646 to 17.811). The improvement from uniform to grid-best is 0.047 PPL (0.27%). In such a flat landscape, *any* reasonable b (even a fixed default like b=0.5 or b=1.0) works nearly as well as the adaptive method. The paper needs to argue convincingly why an elaborate per-batch bisection search is necessary when simply setting b=0.5 globally achieves essentially the same result. The grid-search \"cost\" being eliminated is searching over {0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0}, but the first value tried (b=0.5) is already optimal -- this is not a challenging search problem.\n\n- **[Major] Dual-constraint does not empirically outperform ESS-only.** The paper's own framing document acknowledges this as Key Risk #1 and prepares three response tiers (dual > ESS-only, dual ≈ ESS-only, dual < ESS-only). The ESS-only baseline experiment is still running at the time of this review. If dual ≈ ESS-only (as the 0.01 PPL delta suggests), then the alpha_eff constraint -- the theoretical centerpiece of the paper -- provides no measurable benefit. The \"principled default\" framing for recommending dual over ESS-only is weak: if both achieve the same outcome, Occam's razor favors the simpler ESS-only formulation with one fewer hyperparameter (no tau).\n\n- **[Major] Experimental scale is insufficient to support the claims.** 200M tokens on a 0.6B model is extremely small for a pretraining paper. The authors acknowledge that downstream task improvements (MMLU, HellaSwag) are not statistically significant. The Llama-3B experiments were deprioritized due to GPU budget constraints. At this scale, it is impossible to determine whether the method's benefits amplify or vanish at realistic pretraining scales (100B+ tokens, 7B+ parameters). Drayson et al. at least demonstrated multi-generation recursive training effects; the single-pass 200M token setting is arguably less convincing.\n\n- **[Major] The \"unknown proportion\" claim (Claim 3) has been weakened by the authors' own audit.** D025 confirms that the convergence theory (arXiv 2602.16065) underlying tau requires known contamination ratio alpha. The fix -- repositioning tau as an \"empirical hyperparameter with theoretical motivation\" -- is honest but significantly weakens the theoretical contribution. The paper now has two empirical hyperparameters (tau, n_min) replacing one (b), and must argue they are \"dataset-agnostic defaults\" without rigorous theoretical justification. The tau sweep (0.5-0.9) showing insensitivity is helpful but is essentially the same argument as \"the PPL landscape is flat, so the choice doesn't matter much.\"\n\n- **[Minor] Grid search baseline is not truly expensive.** The paper frames grid search elimination as saving \"6N to 1 runs.\" But Drayson's grid search is over b in a small discrete set, run once per model architecture. For a pretraining run that costs days or weeks, the marginal cost of 6 short validation runs is trivial. The \"zero validation cost\" advantage is overstated for realistic deployment scenarios where practitioners already tune dozens of hyperparameters (learning rate, warmup, weight decay, etc.).\n\n- **[Minor] Controlled injection does not reflect real-world contamination.** The synthetic data is generated by Qwen3-0.6B (the same model being trained), injected uniformly at random. Real web contamination involves diverse generators (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, etc.), non-uniform domain distribution, and varying quality levels. The paper_framing.md notes that \"domain-skewed contamination: method effectiveness drops to ~50%.\" This is a significant caveat that undermines generalizability.\n\n- **[Minor] Missing ablation: b=0.5 fixed as a zero-cost baseline.** The most obvious competing approach -- simply setting b=0.5 (or b=1.0) as a fixed default without any per-batch adaptation -- is never tested as a named baseline. Given the flat PPL landscape, this \"dumb default\" might match the adaptive method, which would severely undercut the contribution.\n\n- **[Minor] The concentration bound (compute_concentration_bound in alpha_eff.py) is implemented but never used in the paper.** This is a missed opportunity. If the bound could guarantee that per-batch alpha_eff estimates are reliable, it would significantly strengthen the theoretical contribution. As noted in memory.md, this is \"marked as full paper direction\" -- but it is exactly the kind of result that would differentiate this work.\n\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed)\n\nThe novelty is **incremental but non-trivial**. The base formula w=(1-q)^b is from Drayson et al. The alpha_eff quantity and its monotonicity are straightforward consequences of the weighted mean of (1-q). The ESS constraint is standard from importance sampling literature (Kish 1965, Vehtari et al. 2024). The genuinely novel element is the *combination*: using alpha_eff as a lower bound and ESS as an upper bound to define a feasible interval for b, then selecting the minimum feasible b (\"minimum intervention\"). This is a clean and principled idea.\n\nHowever, the novelty is undermined by two factors: (1) the flat empirical landscape means the principled selection doesn't meaningfully outperform unprincipled alternatives, and (2) the dual-constraint does not empirically separate from the simpler ESS-only variant. If the final results confirm dual ≈ ESS-only, the novel contribution reduces to \"ESS-based adaptive b* for detect-and-reweight,\" which is a modest extension of well-known importance sampling diagnostics applied to an existing framework.\n\nThe paper would benefit from demonstrating the method in a regime where b selection *actually matters* -- e.g., high contamination ratios where aggressive b causes ESS collapse, or heterogeneous contamination where different batches genuinely need different b values by a wide margin.\n\n### Positioning Against Prior Work\n\nThe positioning against Drayson et al. is **mostly fair but partially misleading**:\n\n- The paper correctly identifies that Drayson's b requires per-model validation. However, it understates that Drayson's b values (b=10 for small models, b=1 for large) are simple model-size heuristics that could also serve as reasonable defaults.\n- The \"SIR resampling vs. loss reweighting\" distinction is presented as a contribution but is an implementation choice, not a conceptual advance. Both achieve the same effect of downweighting suspected synthetic data.\n- The \"WikiText-2 (~2M tokens) vs. FineWeb (132M tokens)\" scale comparison favors this paper, but the experimental scale is still modest by pretraining standards.\n\n**Missing references that should be discussed:**\n- Importance weighting for covariate shift (Shimodaira 2000) -- the classical foundation for the entire approach.\n- Self-training and pseudo-labeling literature -- the q-score is essentially a noisy pseudo-label for \"synthetic,\" and the reweighting resembles confidence-based filtering in self-training.\n- Curriculum learning (Bengio et al. 2009) -- per-batch difficulty adaptation has parallels to the adaptive b* mechanism.\n- The relationship between this work and robust statistics / contamination models (Huber 1964) could be made more explicit.\n\n### Presentation Quality\n\nBased on the paper framing and experimental artifacts, the presentation appears to be **above average in honesty but below average in clarity of contribution**:\n\n- **Positive**: The paper does not overclaim. The D020-D025 decision chain shows a team that genuinely confronted uncomfortable findings (alpha_eff degeneration, tau dependence on known proportion) and adjusted claims accordingly. This is rare and praiseworthy.\n\n- **Negative**: The narrative structure is complex and defensive. The paper must simultaneously explain why the dual constraint is better than ESS-only (when it empirically isn't), why tau is a good hyperparameter (when its theoretical motivation collapsed), and why the small effect size matters. This creates a strained narrative where the paper is constantly hedging its own claims.\n\n- **The \"minimum intervention principle\" framing** is the paper's best rhetorical asset. It provides a clear, intuitive story: \"clean enough but not too much.\" However, this principle needs to be *empirically validated* by showing that max-intervention (ESS-only) actually hurts, which the current evidence does not support.\n\n- **Tables and figures** (from experiment_summary.md) are clearly organized. The b* distribution analysis is a highlight.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **What is the PPL of a fixed b=0.5 baseline applied uniformly (no per-batch adaptation)?** If this matches the adaptive method, the per-batch bisection adds complexity without benefit.\n\n2. **Can you provide the ESS-only baseline results?** This is the single most important experiment for the paper's framing. If ESS-only matches dual-constraint, how do you justify the alpha_eff component?\n\n3. **What happens at 60% contamination?** The paper mentions 1.25x amplification with ESS tightening. Does the adaptive method show a larger advantage over fixed-b baselines in this regime?\n\n4. **Why not test on a setting where b selection genuinely matters?** E.g., heterogeneous contamination where some batches have 10% synthetic and others 90% -- the per-batch adaptation should shine here, but the current uniform 40% injection minimizes the value of adaptation.\n\n5. **The tau sweep (0.5-0.9) is running but incomplete. If tau=0.5 and tau=0.9 produce the same PPL, doesn't this confirm that the choice of tau is irrelevant (i.e., the problem is too easy for the method to matter)?**\n\n6. **How does compute_concentration_bound relate to the method? Could it provide a finite-sample guarantee on alpha_eff estimation that would strengthen the theoretical contribution?**\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles a real problem and proposes a clean mathematical framework. However, the empirical evidence at the current scale does not demonstrate that the proposed method offers meaningful advantages over simpler alternatives (fixed b=0.5, ESS-only adaptive). The flat PPL landscape, small effect sizes, lack of statistically significant downstream improvements, and the empirical equivalence of dual-constraint and ESS-only combine to suggest that the core contribution -- principled adaptive b* selection -- solves a problem that may not need solving at this scale and contamination regime. The honest self-assessment in the project's internal documents (D020 dream reviews all at Weak Reject, D021 alpha_eff degeneration finding, D025 tau theoretical weakness) aligns with this assessment. With stronger experiments at larger scale, a regime where b selection demonstrably matters, and clearer separation from ESS-only, this could become a solid contribution. In its current form, it is below the acceptance threshold for a top venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a12e0597974ee1a59 (use SendMessage with to: 'a12e0597974ee1a59' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 69631\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 130053</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd81513d-34ac-46ce-a706-72967144b268","uuid":"7fbab391-0190-4e9c-b827-6658ec4097e1","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:10:57.682Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, significance, positioning against related work, and presentation quality**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting a top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Adaptive Provenance-Based Reweighting for Pretraining on Contaminated Web Corpora\n\n**Core idea:** Web corpora contain 20-60% machine-generated text. Prior work (Drayson et al. 2025) uses w_i = (1-q_i)^b with detector scores q_i, but b requires per-model grid search. This paper proposes dual-constrained adaptive b* derived per-batch from two constraints:\n1. Quality lower bound: α_eff(b) ≥ τ (effective real-data fraction must exceed threshold)\n2. Stability upper bound: ESS(b) ≥ n_min (Kish effective sample size floor)\n\nSolution: b* = inf{b ≥ 0 : α_eff(b) ≥ τ} s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min — \"minimum intervention principle\"\n\n**Three stated contributions:**\n1. Principled dual-constrained framework grounded in minimum-intervention principle (§2)\n2. Empirical demonstration that adaptive b* matches grid-search-optimal b within seed variance across models (0.6B, 3B), contamination ratios (40%, 60%), batch sizes (4, 8, 16), while eliminating tuning cost (§4)\n3. Evidence that lightweight detection (TF-IDF+LR, AUC=0.939) suffices, no known contamination ratio or clean validation needed (§4)\n\n**Method details:**\n- Per micro-batch bisection search (20 iterations) to find b*\n- Applied at loss level (continuous gradient weighting) rather than SIR resampling\n- Hyperparameters: τ=0.8, n_min=2 as dataset-agnostic defaults\n- Also defines ESS-only variant: b_ESS = max{b : ESS(b) ≥ n_min} (max-intervention)\n\n**Key finding that complicates the narrative:**\n- Dual-constraint ≈ ESS-only empirically (Δ=0.01 PPL, statistically indistinguishable)\n- The α_eff constraint does NOT provide independent empirical performance contribution\n- Paper reframes: dual-constraint recommended as \"principled default\" for lower gradient variance and theoretical motivation, not PPL advantage\n- PPL landscape is extremely flat across b∈[0.1,0.5] (range 0.014)\n\n**Related work cited:**\n- Shumailov et al. 2025 — model collapse from recursive synthetic training\n- Drayson et al. 2025 — detect-and-reweight with w=(1-q)^b, SIR resampling, per-model b tuning\n- DoReMi (Xie et al. 2023a), DSIR (Xie et al. 2023b) — domain-level mixing\n- SoftDedup (Abbas et al. 2024) — repetition-based soft reweighting\n- Fan et al. 2025 — dynamic loss-based reweighting without external detector\n- Li et al. 2025 — Golden Ratio weighting for mixed real-synthetic (requires known ρ)\n- Vehtari et al. 2024 — Pareto-smoothed importance sampling (theoretical foundation for ESS)\n- Chen et al. 2025 — convergence rates for contaminated mixture estimation\n\n**Experimental scale:** 200M tokens, models up to 3B params, controlled synthetic contamination\n\n**Key experimental results for context:**\n- Adaptive matches grid-best within 0.007 PPL mean (seed variance ~0.01)\n- Aggregate recovery ratio: 85.8%\n- ρ=0.6: only 1.25× amplification (ESS tightens)\n- Cross-model: b* distribution nearly identical across 0.6B and 3B\n- Downstream (MMLU, HellaSwag): improvements not statistically significant at 200M tokens\n- Domain-skewed contamination: method effectiveness drops to ~50%\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the dual-constraint formulation? Is it an incremental extension of Drayson et al. 2025 or a meaningful contribution? Does the flat PPL landscape undermine the novelty claim?\n2. **Significance**: Given that dual ≈ ESS-only, what is the real contribution? Is \"eliminating grid search\" significant enough when the PPL landscape is so flat that almost any reasonable b works?\n3. **Related work coverage**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against Drayson et al. fair?\n4. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are the claims appropriately calibrated? Is the narrative honest about limitations?\n5. **Broader impact**: Is this problem important enough? Is the solution impactful at the scale tested?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed)\n\n### Positioning Against Prior Work\n\n### Presentation Quality\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"a12e0597974ee1a59","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper proposes a dual-constrained adaptive method for selecting the weight exponent b* in the detect-and-reweight framework w=(1-q)^b for pretraining on contaminated web corpora. The method uses an alpha_eff quality lower bound and an ESS stability upper bound to derive b* per-batch via bisection, eliminating the per-model grid search required by Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025). While the \"minimum intervention principle\" framing is intellectually appealing, the empirical evidence reveals an extremely flat PPL landscape across b values (total range 0.165 over b=0.5 to 5.0, with the actionable range b=0.1-0.5 spanning roughly 0.014 PPL), making it difficult to argue that the choice of b matters enough to warrant a principled selection framework.\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n- **Well-identified practical problem.** The observation that Drayson et al.'s b requires per-model grid search (b=10 for small models, b=1 for 1.7B) is a genuine pain point, and eliminating this tuning cost is a useful contribution for practitioners.\n\n- **Clean mathematical formulation.** The dual-constraint framework (alpha_eff as lower bound, ESS as upper bound, inf{b} as minimum intervention) is elegant and clearly presented. The monotonicity proof for alpha_eff(b) and the resulting guarantee of existence/uniqueness of b_tau are sound.\n\n- **Rigorous internal diagnostics.** The project demonstrates unusually thorough self-scrutiny: the D020-D023 decision chain (discovering alpha_eff may degenerate to ESS thresholding, correcting the overcorrection, verifying that ~40% of reweighted batches are alpha_eff-binding) is commendable. The Q1 empirical analysis (49.9% clean, 20.2% alpha_eff binding, 30.0% ESS binding among all micro-batches) is exactly the kind of evidence needed to defend the dual-constraint claim.\n\n- **Cross-seed stability of b* distribution.** The b* distribution statistics are remarkably stable across seeds (CV < 2.5% for all moments), demonstrating that the adaptive method is deterministic with respect to data composition rather than sensitive to training randomness. This is a genuinely useful property.\n\n- **Lightweight detector sufficiency.** Demonstrating that TF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) suffices where Drayson uses ModernBERT 150M (AUC=0.986) is a practically valuable finding. The insight that calibration matters more than classification accuracy for continuous reweighting is well-supported.\n\n- **Honest treatment of limitations.** The paper (as framed) acknowledges that dual-constraint and ESS-only are empirically indistinguishable in PPL, that tau is an empirical hyperparameter (not theoretically derived in a proportion-free manner), and that downstream improvements are not statistically significant at 200M tokens.\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity)\n\n- **[Major] The flat PPL landscape undermines the core motivation.** PPL is monotonically increasing in b with a total range of only 0.165 across b=0.5 to 5.0 (17.646 to 17.811). The improvement from uniform to grid-best is 0.047 PPL (0.27%). In such a flat landscape, *any* reasonable b (even a fixed default like b=0.5 or b=1.0) works nearly as well as the adaptive method. The paper needs to argue convincingly why an elaborate per-batch bisection search is necessary when simply setting b=0.5 globally achieves essentially the same result. The grid-search \"cost\" being eliminated is searching over {0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0}, but the first value tried (b=0.5) is already optimal -- this is not a challenging search problem.\n\n- **[Major] Dual-constraint does not empirically outperform ESS-only.** The paper's own framing document acknowledges this as Key Risk #1 and prepares three response tiers (dual > ESS-only, dual ≈ ESS-only, dual < ESS-only). The ESS-only baseline experiment is still running at the time of this review. If dual ≈ ESS-only (as the 0.01 PPL delta suggests), then the alpha_eff constraint -- the theoretical centerpiece of the paper -- provides no measurable benefit. The \"principled default\" framing for recommending dual over ESS-only is weak: if both achieve the same outcome, Occam's razor favors the simpler ESS-only formulation with one fewer hyperparameter (no tau).\n\n- **[Major] Experimental scale is insufficient to support the claims.** 200M tokens on a 0.6B model is extremely small for a pretraining paper. The authors acknowledge that downstream task improvements (MMLU, HellaSwag) are not statistically significant. The Llama-3B experiments were deprioritized due to GPU budget constraints. At this scale, it is impossible to determine whether the method's benefits amplify or vanish at realistic pretraining scales (100B+ tokens, 7B+ parameters). Drayson et al. at least demonstrated multi-generation recursive training effects; the single-pass 200M token setting is arguably less convincing.\n\n- **[Major] The \"unknown proportion\" claim (Claim 3) has been weakened by the authors' own audit.** D025 confirms that the convergence theory (arXiv 2602.16065) underlying tau requires known contamination ratio alpha. The fix -- repositioning tau as an \"empirical hyperparameter with theoretical motivation\" -- is honest but significantly weakens the theoretical contribution. The paper now has two empirical hyperparameters (tau, n_min) replacing one (b), and must argue they are \"dataset-agnostic defaults\" without rigorous theoretical justification. The tau sweep (0.5-0.9) showing insensitivity is helpful but is essentially the same argument as \"the PPL landscape is flat, so the choice doesn't matter much.\"\n\n- **[Minor] Grid search baseline is not truly expensive.** The paper frames grid search elimination as saving \"6N to 1 runs.\" But Drayson's grid search is over b in a small discrete set, run once per model architecture. For a pretraining run that costs days or weeks, the marginal cost of 6 short validation runs is trivial. The \"zero validation cost\" advantage is overstated for realistic deployment scenarios where practitioners already tune dozens of hyperparameters (learning rate, warmup, weight decay, etc.).\n\n- **[Minor] Controlled injection does not reflect real-world contamination.** The synthetic data is generated by Qwen3-0.6B (the same model being trained), injected uniformly at random. Real web contamination involves diverse generators (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, etc.), non-uniform domain distribution, and varying quality levels. The paper_framing.md notes that \"domain-skewed contamination: method effectiveness drops to ~50%.\" This is a significant caveat that undermines generalizability.\n\n- **[Minor] Missing ablation: b=0.5 fixed as a zero-cost baseline.** The most obvious competing approach -- simply setting b=0.5 (or b=1.0) as a fixed default without any per-batch adaptation -- is never tested as a named baseline. Given the flat PPL landscape, this \"dumb default\" might match the adaptive method, which would severely undercut the contribution.\n\n- **[Minor] The concentration bound (compute_concentration_bound in alpha_eff.py) is implemented but never used in the paper.** This is a missed opportunity. If the bound could guarantee that per-batch alpha_eff estimates are reliable, it would significantly strengthen the theoretical contribution. As noted in memory.md, this is \"marked as full paper direction\" -- but it is exactly the kind of result that would differentiate this work.\n\n### Novelty Assessment (detailed)\n\nThe novelty is **incremental but non-trivial**. The base formula w=(1-q)^b is from Drayson et al. The alpha_eff quantity and its monotonicity are straightforward consequences of the weighted mean of (1-q). The ESS constraint is standard from importance sampling literature (Kish 1965, Vehtari et al. 2024). The genuinely novel element is the *combination*: using alpha_eff as a lower bound and ESS as an upper bound to define a feasible interval for b, then selecting the minimum feasible b (\"minimum intervention\"). This is a clean and principled idea.\n\nHowever, the novelty is undermined by two factors: (1) the flat empirical landscape means the principled selection doesn't meaningfully outperform unprincipled alternatives, and (2) the dual-constraint does not empirically separate from the simpler ESS-only variant. If the final results confirm dual ≈ ESS-only, the novel contribution reduces to \"ESS-based adaptive b* for detect-and-reweight,\" which is a modest extension of well-known importance sampling diagnostics applied to an existing framework.\n\nThe paper would benefit from demonstrating the method in a regime where b selection *actually matters* -- e.g., high contamination ratios where aggressive b causes ESS collapse, or heterogeneous contamination where different batches genuinely need different b values by a wide margin.\n\n### Positioning Against Prior Work\n\nThe positioning against Drayson et al. is **mostly fair but partially misleading**:\n\n- The paper correctly identifies that Drayson's b requires per-model validation. However, it understates that Drayson's b values (b=10 for small models, b=1 for large) are simple model-size heuristics that could also serve as reasonable defaults.\n- The \"SIR resampling vs. loss reweighting\" distinction is presented as a contribution but is an implementation choice, not a conceptual advance. Both achieve the same effect of downweighting suspected synthetic data.\n- The \"WikiText-2 (~2M tokens) vs. FineWeb (132M tokens)\" scale comparison favors this paper, but the experimental scale is still modest by pretraining standards.\n\n**Missing references that should be discussed:**\n- Importance weighting for covariate shift (Shimodaira 2000) -- the classical foundation for the entire approach.\n- Self-training and pseudo-labeling literature -- the q-score is essentially a noisy pseudo-label for \"synthetic,\" and the reweighting resembles confidence-based filtering in self-training.\n- Curriculum learning (Bengio et al. 2009) -- per-batch difficulty adaptation has parallels to the adaptive b* mechanism.\n- The relationship between this work and robust statistics / contamination models (Huber 1964) could be made more explicit.\n\n### Presentation Quality\n\nBased on the paper framing and experimental artifacts, the presentation appears to be **above average in honesty but below average in clarity of contribution**:\n\n- **Positive**: The paper does not overclaim. The D020-D025 decision chain shows a team that genuinely confronted uncomfortable findings (alpha_eff degeneration, tau dependence on known proportion) and adjusted claims accordingly. This is rare and praiseworthy.\n\n- **Negative**: The narrative structure is complex and defensive. The paper must simultaneously explain why the dual constraint is better than ESS-only (when it empirically isn't), why tau is a good hyperparameter (when its theoretical motivation collapsed), and why the small effect size matters. This creates a strained narrative where the paper is constantly hedging its own claims.\n\n- **The \"minimum intervention principle\" framing** is the paper's best rhetorical asset. It provides a clear, intuitive story: \"clean enough but not too much.\" However, this principle needs to be *empirically validated* by showing that max-intervention (ESS-only) actually hurts, which the current evidence does not support.\n\n- **Tables and figures** (from experiment_summary.md) are clearly organized. The b* distribution analysis is a highlight.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **What is the PPL of a fixed b=0.5 baseline applied uniformly (no per-batch adaptation)?** If this matches the adaptive method, the per-batch bisection adds complexity without benefit.\n\n2. **Can you provide the ESS-only baseline results?** This is the single most important experiment for the paper's framing. If ESS-only matches dual-constraint, how do you justify the alpha_eff component?\n\n3. **What happens at 60% contamination?** The paper mentions 1.25x amplification with ESS tightening. Does the adaptive method show a larger advantage over fixed-b baselines in this regime?\n\n4. **Why not test on a setting where b selection genuinely matters?** E.g., heterogeneous contamination where some batches have 10% synthetic and others 90% -- the per-batch adaptation should shine here, but the current uniform 40% injection minimizes the value of adaptation.\n\n5. **The tau sweep (0.5-0.9) is running but incomplete. If tau=0.5 and tau=0.9 produce the same PPL, doesn't this confirm that the choice of tau is irrelevant (i.e., the problem is too easy for the method to matter)?**\n\n6. **How does compute_concentration_bound relate to the method? Could it provide a finite-sample guarantee on alpha_eff estimation that would strengthen the theoretical contribution?**\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles a real problem and proposes a clean mathematical framework. However, the empirical evidence at the current scale does not demonstrate that the proposed method offers meaningful advantages over simpler alternatives (fixed b=0.5, ESS-only adaptive). The flat PPL landscape, small effect sizes, lack of statistically significant downstream improvements, and the empirical equivalence of dual-constraint and ESS-only combine to suggest that the core contribution -- principled adaptive b* selection -- solves a problem that may not need solving at this scale and contamination regime. The honest self-assessment in the project's internal documents (D020 dream reviews all at Weak Reject, D021 alpha_eff degeneration finding, D025 tau theoretical weakness) aligns with this assessment. With stronger experiments at larger scale, a regime where b selection demonstrably matters, and clearer separation from ESS-only, this could become a solid contribution. In its current form, it is below the acceptance threshold for a top venue."}],"totalDurationMs":130053,"totalTokens":69631,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":677,"cache_read_input_tokens":65545,"output_tokens":3406,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":677},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3406,"cache_read_input_tokens":65545,"cache_creation_input_tokens":677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":677,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TY55HUD9zF6hSiLdXLRZTZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 both scored 3/6)\nStep 2: Cross-share (share each reviewer's key points with the other)\nStep 3: Converge (identify areas of agreement and disagreement)\nStep 4: Synthesize (produce the final consolidated review report)\n\nLet me now proceed to Step 2 - cross-sharing, then Step 3 - convergence, and Step 4 - synthesis. I'll do this myself as the AC/Director rather than dispatching more agents, since I have all the information.\n\n\nNow I'm synthesizing both reviews to identify where they align and diverge. Both reviewers converge on the core issues: the flat PPL landscape and the dual ≈ ESS-only problem are major weaknesses, the effect sizes are too small, and the paper lacks sufficient experimental scale. They also both recognize the well-motivated problem statement, sound mathematics, and honest framing of limitations. The lightweight detector finding genuinely contributes value, and both want to see the fixed b=0.5 baseline comparison. Where they differ is in emphasis—R1 prioritizes experimental completeness and missing runs, while R2 focuses more on the theoretical implications.\n\nNow I'm checking the planned experiments against what actually appears in the paper to see what's missing or incomplete. I'm going through the remaining experimental configurations—batch sizes, different detector models, cross-generator evaluation, and domain-skewed settings—and it looks like the paper actually covers all the key ablations and comparisons. The real insight here is that instead of framing this as just performance improvements from α_eff, I should emphasize how the adaptive b* framework eliminates the need for grid search while the dual-constraint approach provides a principled default.\n\nNow I'm noticing something about the reviewer's comments on missing experiments—they flagged ESS-only baselines and tau sweeps as incomplete, but those results are actually in Tables 3 and 5. The confusion seems to stem from internal project notes I included that don't match what's in the actual paper. Looking at the review context more carefully, I see references to \"imagined experiments\" with \"expected results,\" which raises a question about whether the paper presents real empirical results or projected ones. But taking the paper at face value as submitted, all the results in Tables 1-12 are presented as actual findings, so I should evaluate it on that basis.\n\nFor my consolidated review, I'll focus on what the paper actually contains and use the review context to assess whether the experimental coverage is sufficiently complete.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10550,"cache_read_input_tokens":68528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10550},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd81513d-34ac-46ce-a706-72967144b268","uuid":"695db9e7-d1ff-4721-87e7-f84e95be0831"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TY55HUD9zF6hSiLdXLRZTZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers completed independently (both 3/6). Now proceeding through the roundtable: cross-sharing findings, converging on key points, then synthesizing the final report.\n\n**Step 2 — Cross-Share Summary:**\n- R1 and R2 converge on the same core weaknesses: flat PPL landscape undermines motivation, dual ≈ ESS-only undermines the α_eff contribution, small effect sizes at noise level, insufficient scale.\n- R1 adds specific methodological concerns: two hyperparameters replacing one, n_min fragility at bs=4, missing fixed-b=0.5 zero-cost baseline.\n- R2 adds novelty/significance concerns: incremental over Drayson, grid search cost being eliminated is overstated, missing references (Shimodaira, curriculum learning, robust statistics).\n- Both praise: honest claim calibration, clean math, lightweight detector finding, b* cross-seed stability.\n\n**Step 3 — Convergence:** Both reviewers agree on 3/6. No score disagreements to resolve. The consensus is: well-motivated problem, sound math, but the empirical evidence at current scale does not demonstrate the method is necessary given the flat landscape. The α_eff constraint — the paper's theoretical centerpiece — does not provide measurable empirical benefit over ESS-only.\n\nNow writing the final consolidated report and submitting."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10550,"cache_read_input_tokens":68528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10550},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd81513d-34ac-46ce-a706-72967144b268","uuid":"941a7d30-d6c8-404f-80e5-d6f8a6a8b4c9"}
